WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.853 --> 00:00:06.270
OLGA MIERZWA-SULIMA:
Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.060
I'm really excited to be here.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:10.730
So let's start.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:14.420
I personally believe that
the future of knowledge work

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:16.520
will eventually be remote.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.120
So in some sense, we all
got to experience the future

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.540
in the last few months.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:26.140
However, this might not have
been what you hoped for,

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.450
because setting up a
collaborative environment

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:31.180
for your data science team
can be challenging, even when

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.780
working side by
side in an office.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.170
At Appsilon, we spent years
developing efficient systems

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:39.970
for remote collaboration.

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:43.390
And in this presentation, I
will cover the best practices

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.670
that work for us.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:50.140
So I'm Olga
Mierzwa-Sulima, and I'm

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:52.970
a Senior Scientist and
an experienced Project

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:54.440
Leader of Appsilon.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.870
I have a background
in econometrics,

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:02.270
and my main technological
stack at work is R.

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:04.910
I have been building
our community here

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:09.930
in Warsaw since 2014.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:14.180
And I plan to cover
three areas in my talk.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.790
So project, management doing
effective code reviews,

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:23.150
and setting up a painless
development environment in R.

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:26.210
So when it comes to
project management, almost

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.780
all of the projects
that we run in Appsilon

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:34.020
are managed with scrum
approach with small variations.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.180
At the beginning
of the project, we

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.850
sit down together
with our client

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.040
and collect the
requirements that

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.180
are translated to high level
tasks in the project backlog.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:49.470
At this stage, we also
provide a high level estimate

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.420
on how much time
we think we need.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.840
For example, we contract
work for eight weeks, each

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.990
with one week long sprint.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:03.000
Every sprint starts with
a sprint planning, where

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.730
the dev team and
the client select

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.310
the task to work
in a given week,

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.490
and we also set the varieties.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:15.270
During the week, we meet
daily inside the dev team

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.040
to give each other updates.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:21.480
We also use those
meetings as an opportunity

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:22.980
to catch up on the
small things that

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.740
are happening inside
the team, because we

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:29.510
don't have those random meetings
in an office that happen next

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:31.860
to the coffee machine.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.410
The rest of the communication
during the week,

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:38.160
we try to do a sync
using a dedicated project

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.600
channel on Slack.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.080
We also use Slack to
give each other updates,

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.410
such as that we start or
finish our work or are

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.910
in the Do Not Disturb mode.

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:53.430
We also hold one-on-ones
between team members

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:57.660
to discuss specific problems
related to the project.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:01.960
But coming back to
our Scrum process.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:05.880
At the end of the week,
we do the sprint review

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.940
where do we show to our
client the increment.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:11.760
Usually, this
means doing a demo,

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.710
showing the app
with new features,

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:18.540
or going through a notebook, and
it validates new data science

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:19.770
hypothesis.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:23.100
We also hold
retrospective meetings

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.800
to celebrate things
that went well

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.300
or to discuss
potential improvements.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:32.440
We use to Project Board
to help us manage backlog.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:37.330
We usually-- we usually use
tools available from GitHub,

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:40.040
Asana, or Jira.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:43.210
So [INAUDIBLE] development is
standard and common practice.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:45.700
But making it work
for your team is a--

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:46.760
is a clue.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.410
So we make sure to always
have well organized

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:53.200
project board that is
reflecting-- reflecting

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.560
the current status of work.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.570
So basically, if I look
into the project report,

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:02.380
it should tell me who is working
on what tasks and what are

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:04.120
the varieties.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.460
Also, with Appsilon,
we have this rule

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:08.980
that all work needs
to be documented.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:11.380
So whatever you are
doing, there should

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.870
be a corresponding
task describing it

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:15.700
on the project report.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:19.760
The task needs to have a clear
and informative description,

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.570
which is understandable
not only to the best team,

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:25.990
but also to our clients
as they have access

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:28.270
to the project report as well.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:30.760
We introduced an
implementation plan,

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:34.120
which is something that is
non-standard to a classical

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.560
[INAUDIBLE] field.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:39.070
Basically, the person
who is doing the task

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:42.610
has to provide a description
how they will approach the task

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:45.400
and get an acceptance
from another team member

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:49.240
before starting the
actual implementation.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:53.230
This way we make sure that any
proposed change makes sense,

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:56.030
and we verify it early enough.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:05:00.090
Finally, the reviewer
is responsible to check

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:06.310
if the solution corresponds
with task requirement.

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:09.400
As you probably know this,
our product management flow

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.250
is tightly related
to code review.

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:14.080
I will assume that
majority of you

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.590
are familiar with that
concept, and I will only

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:21.460
talk what makes it effective.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:24.520
We have a checklist that is
part of our definition of done

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:27.820
for a task and includes
the following points.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:30.220
All the code needs
to be peer-reviewed.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:32.660
And there are no
exceptions from this rule.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:35.500
So even fixing a typo
in the documentation

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.680
needs to be peer-reviewed.

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:42.010
We also make sure that
continuous integration checks,

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:44.680
such as linter, unit
test, integration tests,

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:47.870
are configured and passed.

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:51.020
Next, any added
or modified codes

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:52.910
has to follow our style guide.

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:55.100
This is especially
important because it

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:59.180
allows us to ensure quality,
the more effective code reviews,

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:01.100
learn from each
other, and [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:03.580
back in the code more easily.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:06.010
Next, all the changes--

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:11.040
and it needs to be tested
manually or automated test.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.400
Further, we need to make sure
that no new errors or warnings

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:18.600
have been introduced and the
ReadMe documentation and code

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:23.760
comment have been updated
according to the change.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.420
We try to automate as
many things as possible.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:31.320
And we use continuous
integration with GitHub Actions

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:36.150
to perform automated checks and
let the machines do the job.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:39.270
For example, we
automatically run unit tests

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:42.880
after every code push.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:47.340
We also use project templates
to initialize the repo structure

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:51.130
for typical project types and
use full of request templates

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:53.800
that contains a definition
of the checklist

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:56.931
from the previous slide to
help a reviewer do the review.

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:03.520
So finally, we can talk about
the development environment.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:07.260
It's the most crucial part
when working in R. Otherwise,

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:12.060
the same code can give different
results on different machines.

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:14.380
Other projects can be affected.

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:16.980
As in a global environment,
shared packages

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:20.370
can change and crash
unrelated project.

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:23.670
Deployment can give
you a real headache

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:25.680
because establishing
infrastructure

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:28.300
can be challenging. if
it's not being tracked.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:31.500
And last but not the
least, your team members

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.440
will waste time setting
up an environment

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.780
rather than jumping
straight to work.

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:40.740
This might not be a problem
when starting a new project.

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:44.130
But imagine adding a new
team member in the middle

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:46.470
or at the end of the project.

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:48.610
We'll solve all of those issues.

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:53.020
We did a development in RStudio
renv inside a Docker container,

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:56.250
which is fixed and
dedicated per project.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:08:00.630
We leveraged Docker and renv
to control underlying system

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:03.060
dependencies and our packages.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:08.010
Docker and renv package together
make team collaboration easy.

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:10.590
All the changes through
a Docker file or a renv

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:14.880
lockfile that records
packages used in the project.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:18.480
are committed and pushed
through Docker Hub or Github

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:21.390
from where they can be
accessed by other team members.

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:28.930
Previous solution, however,
still requires some level

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:31.360
of skills to set up everything.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:36.970
So some of our clients
use RStudio Server

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:39.710
because it allows
their data science team

00:08:39.710 --> 00:08:43.659
to purely focus on delivering
value using their core

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:47.110
competencies in a highly
secure and flexible development

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:48.091
environment.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:54.120
There is no secret recipe to
make your data science team

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:55.940
work efficiently.

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:58.700
In fact, we found
that using scrum

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:02.270
with well-organized project
board, doing code reviews,

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:04.850
and taking care of the
development environment

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:06.920
is essential for
project [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:11.120
no matter how your
team is structured.

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:13.170
Thank you very much.

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:16.140
And next, my colleague
Pedro will tell you

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:20.070
how to build a dashboard
that can speak for itself.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:21.720
When dealing with
users' adoption,

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:25.100
you can't always
be next to them.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:28.230
You cannot always be next to
them and explain how your app

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:29.110
will work.

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:33.135
It's even more difficult
when working remotely.

00:09:33.135 --> 00:09:38.000
PEDRO SILVA: So I think one
very important thing when

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:42.140
it comes to remote teams is
that you're not always building

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:45.590
dashboards for external users.

00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:48.560
You're sometimes also building
dashboards for your own teams

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:49.550
to work in.

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:53.390
And like Olga said, when
you're in the office

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:56.150
and you're next to
someone, it's very easy

00:09:56.150 --> 00:10:00.360
to just push them in the right
direction of how to use it.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:05.690
But if you don't have this
direct contact with your users,

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:07.070
you end up having--

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:10.100
a good solution for this is
to put a bit of extra work

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:11.870
into your dashboards
and actually

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:15.510
make them speak for themselves.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:18.350
Especially when there's
alternative dashboards,

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:21.380
users typically have a choice.

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:25.470
And they will usually go from
the one that feels better.

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:29.300
So small background from me.

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:31.720
I used to work as
a web developer.

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:33.560
So I used to do pages.

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:35.060
I used to do applications.

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:38.030
And eventually, in the
last couple of years,

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:41.540
I landed in R and in Shiny.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:47.100
So one of the great things that
I ended up learning about Shiny

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:50.930
is that deep down, there's a
lot of JavaScript, a lot of CSS,

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:52.670
a lot of HTML involved.

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:56.240
So this actually allows you to--

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:59.570
allowed me to reuse a lot of
what I used as a web developer

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:03.050
to actually work on
Shiny dashboards.

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:07.670
So why does UI actually matter?

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:10.880
The question is, if you
have a dashboard that works,

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:14.030
why should you actually care
about making it look nicer?

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:18.470
And the truth is
that UI, the way

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:21.110
your dashboard
looks and feels is

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:22.930
very important for your users.

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:26.540
So this is the first experience
that your users are going

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:29.270
to get using your application.

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:33.530
And especially now that
everyone is so used

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:39.620
to the way the internet works,
the way the applications

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:43.340
in your phones and
tablets work, users

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:46.910
expect things to behave
in a specific way.

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:49.760
It's also very important to
notice that users don't usually

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:52.160
care what's in the background.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:54.740
And this is valid even
for some Shiny apps.

00:11:54.740 --> 00:11:57.890
If your users use
the application

00:11:57.890 --> 00:12:02.990
to get a specific insight
or to get some data,

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:06.230
they don't really want
to they don't really

00:12:06.230 --> 00:12:10.250
need to know how complicated
the background process is.

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:12.020
They want to have
a nice experience.

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:18.100
They want to be quick and
efficient at using it.

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:23.520
And in general, you end
up with this concept

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:27.330
that if users don't feel
engaged and comfortable when

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:30.570
they use your application,
they might get frustrated.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:32.040
They might stop using it.

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:36.930
Maybe they'll pick a different
alternative if it's possible.

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:41.970
So instead of going over the
whole UI and what you can do,

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.750
I decided to give you a couple
of things that you can look at,

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:49.850
a couple of parts
of the application

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:53.360
that you can keep
in mind, either when

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:56.390
you're using an application
or you're getting feedback

00:12:56.390 --> 00:12:59.510
or when you're actually
building the application.

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:04.370
So I picked six very important
topics and very important

00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:05.960
points when it comes to UI.

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:09.350
The first one is you should
keep your interface simple.

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:15.350
It means any element that
isn't actually doing anything,

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:17.220
you should probably remove it.

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:19.820
It also means cleaning
up your dashboards

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:24.230
and also means that you pass
a very clear message of what

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:26.170
you're trying your users to--

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:29.060
or what you want your
users to achieve.

00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:34.070
You end up with something that
is very straight to the point,

00:13:34.070 --> 00:13:36.350
because all the elements
there actually serve

00:13:36.350 --> 00:13:38.240
some functionality.

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:41.370
Another important thing
is to be consistent.

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:44.100
So if you have a
text input, reuse it.

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:45.950
If you have a button, reuse it.

00:13:45.950 --> 00:13:50.630
Users kind of learn to
navigate your application,

00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:54.230
so they expect elements to
have the same kind of behavior.

00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:58.730
They expect flows to
work the same way.

00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:01.430
And you can kind
of define patterns

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:04.100
of how your application
flows when you

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:06.990
have very complex applications.

00:14:06.990 --> 00:14:11.200
Another thing to
keep in mind is to be

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:13.450
purposeful in your layout.

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:16.420
This basically means that just
because all the information is

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:21.440
there doesn't mean that it's
being shown in a good way.

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:24.850
So adding white space where
there's no information

00:14:24.850 --> 00:14:26.560
is important.

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:29.590
Using the UI elements
to actually create

00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:32.570
some kind of structure, and
in the case of dashboards,

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:37.600
this is the sidebar
navigation, the top navigation.

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:41.680
All of these can help you
create a bit of structure when

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:43.550
it comes to your dashboard.

00:14:43.550 --> 00:14:45.940
It also means that
you can draw attention

00:14:45.940 --> 00:14:47.890
to important information.

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:49.720
You can you can use colors.

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:52.360
You can use the
size of the elements

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:55.750
to actually change the way
that your users perceive

00:14:55.750 --> 00:14:58.510
parts of it.

00:14:58.510 --> 00:15:00.950
Other one is you--

00:15:00.950 --> 00:15:03.640
a few really good
things that you

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:09.260
can manipulate when it comes
to any kind of web application.

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:11.650
These are color,
texture, and typography.

00:15:11.650 --> 00:15:14.920
So work with these.

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:17.770
Don't overdo it when
it comes to color,

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:20.920
when it comes to contrast,
when it comes to shadows.

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:27.060
Use them to try to attract
your users to specific points

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:32.190
of the application or to make
things less evident and less

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:35.070
important from your application.

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:38.560
Remember that different fonts
send different messages.

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:45.810
So old, large fonts
are a different message

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:51.990
from small, very non-bright
fonts for example.

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:56.430
And also remember that the size
of the fonts, the way the text

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:59.310
is arranged, it
really helps people

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:01.080
go through your
application and be

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:04.070
more efficient at their work.

00:16:04.070 --> 00:16:07.520
Also remember to
provide feedback.

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:10.590
You want your users to
always know where they are,

00:16:10.590 --> 00:16:14.090
what they're doing, if
the application is still

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:15.920
doing something or not.

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:19.820
Using messages,
progress bars, anything

00:16:19.820 --> 00:16:23.750
that gives some kind of
feedback of what's happening,

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:25.910
really helps reducing
the frustration,

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:28.100
especially when you
have scripts that

00:16:28.100 --> 00:16:30.740
are running for very
long timers or you're

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:32.670
uploading a lot of data.

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:35.360
You don't want years of to
think that app just crashed

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:37.610
or that something is wrong.

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:40.934
Keep the [AUDIO OUT]

00:16:56.403 --> 00:16:57.920
SAM: Uh-oh.

00:16:57.920 --> 00:17:00.300
Looks like we might have
lost audio for a second.

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:07.170
Let's see.

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:08.471
Pedro, are you able to hear us.

00:17:08.471 --> 00:17:10.179
Looks like we might
have lost your audio.

00:17:23.170 --> 00:17:24.310
Let's see.

00:17:24.310 --> 00:17:27.340
I'm not-- oh,
Pedro, are you back?

00:17:27.340 --> 00:17:29.850
PEDRO SILVA: Yes, I'm sorry.

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:32.270
SAM: We lost your
audio for a minute.

00:17:32.270 --> 00:17:33.460
PEDRO SILVA: Oh.

00:17:33.460 --> 00:17:37.130
Do you want me to go
back for a second or?

00:17:37.130 --> 00:17:40.090
SAM: Yeah, we missed
the past, like,

00:17:40.090 --> 00:17:42.060
two minutes of what
you were saying.

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:43.350
PEDRO SILVA: OK.

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:44.500
So some--

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:48.440
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:50.810
PEDRO SILVA: From where sorry?

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:52.260
ALEX K. GOLD: Yeah, this slide.

00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:52.927
PEDRO SILVA: OK.

00:17:52.927 --> 00:17:57.110
So I was saying that it's
also very important to provide

00:17:57.110 --> 00:17:59.600
feedback to your users.

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:09.190
This is-- you don't want your
users to know that's your app--

00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:13.130
you want users to be aware of
what's happening with the app.

00:18:13.130 --> 00:18:16.600
This usually means
using progress bars,

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:19.720
using messages,
using notifications.

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:22.480
You should always provide
some kind of feedback

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:25.720
to make sure that your
users don't get lost

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:29.140
or are waiting for some
very long process, some very

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:30.820
long script that's
running, and they

00:18:30.820 --> 00:18:33.370
have no idea what's happening.

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:35.050
If you have something
that you know

00:18:35.050 --> 00:18:39.650
is going to take more than a
few seconds, add a progress bar.

00:18:39.650 --> 00:18:42.400
If you have something that
can be done in the background

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:48.850
whether you're using the
application, make it--

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:53.170
maybe show a notification
when it's over.

00:18:53.170 --> 00:18:55.540
All of this constant
feedback really

00:18:55.540 --> 00:18:58.760
helps when it comes to
the user experience.

00:18:58.760 --> 00:19:02.350
And finally, remember to think
about the default values.

00:19:02.350 --> 00:19:08.380
Shinydashboard sleeve of
inputs of user inputs.

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:10.690
And a lot of the
times these inputs

00:19:10.690 --> 00:19:13.600
can be defaulted to
some values that are

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:15.460
very common for users to use.

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:17.050
If you have a
dropdown but you know

00:19:17.050 --> 00:19:20.120
that's most commonly-- this
is the value that's used,

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:23.470
add the value to that dropdown,
or pre-select that dropdown.

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:26.590
If you have a checkbox that
most of the time is selected,

00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:28.960
select it by default.
Anything that

00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:31.840
avoids unnecessary
actions by the user

00:19:31.840 --> 00:19:36.750
and can make the experience
much faster and much smoother.

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:38.940
So how do you actually
achieve all of these things?

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:44.330
So there's two ways
of going at it.

00:19:44.330 --> 00:19:48.540
The easy and quick way
is use UI packages.

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:53.210
So we're all familiar with
Shiny and its default UI.

00:19:53.210 --> 00:19:57.510
But you can for example
use shinydashboard

00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:59.900
to completely change the
way the application looks.

00:19:59.900 --> 00:20:02.720
And some of these
UI tips are already

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:05.570
implemented in the
actual elements

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:07.950
provided by shinydashboard.

00:20:07.950 --> 00:20:11.120
Another good package
is shiny.semantic.

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:14.420
This is base on a
different CSS framework,

00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:17.480
but it also comes
with a lot of elements

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:24.260
that you don't really need to
think about some of these tips.

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:25.970
But you already
have some elements

00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:28.130
that are already included.

00:20:28.130 --> 00:20:29.690
Another option is
that you can just

00:20:29.690 --> 00:20:36.040
create your own layout,
your own styles.

00:20:36.040 --> 00:20:41.950
And I am giving you here
three very common examples

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:42.640
that I use.

00:20:42.640 --> 00:20:48.280
So maybe you found some
HTML template online

00:20:48.280 --> 00:20:49.730
that you would like to use.

00:20:49.730 --> 00:20:51.970
Use HTML templates--
this is a function that

00:20:51.970 --> 00:20:58.840
just lets you import a full HTML
file to your Shiny application.

00:20:58.840 --> 00:21:00.420
Do you want to style something?

00:21:00.420 --> 00:21:01.840
Use CSS.

00:21:01.840 --> 00:21:03.730
If you have a lot
of styles and want

00:21:03.730 --> 00:21:07.180
to go very deep
into the changes,

00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:09.190
you can even try SASS,
which is basically

00:21:09.190 --> 00:21:11.690
a preprocessor for CSS.

00:21:11.690 --> 00:21:14.410
CSS for developers, lets say.

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:16.460
Do you want to add
custom behavior that

00:21:16.460 --> 00:21:18.340
isn't default out of the box?

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:20.110
If you're building
something very complex,

00:21:20.110 --> 00:21:21.910
you can try HTML widgets.

00:21:21.910 --> 00:21:23.440
But for very simple
behaviors, you

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:26.590
can even use just
Vanilla JavaScript

00:21:26.590 --> 00:21:28.855
with a couple of actions.

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:36.870
And these are some examples
of very popular packages

00:21:36.870 --> 00:21:39.090
that were done using HTML,
which is for example.

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:44.520
So you can see that these are
standards for what we currently

00:21:44.520 --> 00:21:45.900
use as developers.

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:50.770
And they are very
actually very easy to use.

00:21:50.770 --> 00:21:53.220
So I'll just quickly finish
with a couple of examples

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:55.760
of what you can actually do.

00:21:55.760 --> 00:22:00.840
This is an example of just
a dashboard that started

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:03.900
as a simple Shiny dashboard.

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:06.750
We then implemented-- we
then added shiny.semantic,

00:22:06.750 --> 00:22:09.950
a bit of custom styling, and we
