00:23:58.130 --> 00:24:00.440
I have worked with many
different languages,

00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:04.520
like Scala, C#, Python,
C++, and JavaScript.

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:06.770
And I always like to
combine all the knowledge

00:24:06.770 --> 00:24:09.950
from different technologies
and apply it to R and Shiny.

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:12.020
I must say that
throughout my experience,

00:24:12.020 --> 00:24:15.890
I have never seen a
language with a library that

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:19.970
are so fast to build great
looking applications that

00:24:19.970 --> 00:24:22.410
are then used in production.

00:24:22.410 --> 00:24:25.285
So short agenda-- I am going
to start with any introduction.

00:24:25.285 --> 00:24:26.660
Then, I will give
you an overview

00:24:26.660 --> 00:24:29.780
of the scaling and some
details on how you make--

00:24:29.780 --> 00:24:32.770
how you can make your
applications faster

00:24:32.770 --> 00:24:36.120
with just a few simple rules.

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:37.640
So this is you and your team.

00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:39.690
Thanks to all the talk
and to your knowledge,

00:24:39.690 --> 00:24:43.460
you already built the
workflow for your team.

00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:47.870
And knowing Shiny and making
your application beautiful,

00:24:47.870 --> 00:24:51.650
you deploy an application that
is available in production

00:24:51.650 --> 00:24:52.920
for your users.

00:24:52.920 --> 00:24:56.050
And then you start
having people using it.

00:24:56.050 --> 00:24:57.450
First, this is one person.

00:24:57.450 --> 00:24:59.480
Suddenly, there is a
little bit more of them.

00:24:59.480 --> 00:25:00.560
And you get the feedback.

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:03.410
And I really like this
slide, because it sums up

00:25:03.410 --> 00:25:07.160
our experience when we
talk to different clients.

00:25:07.160 --> 00:25:11.570
There is always someone who
created this new fresh change

00:25:11.570 --> 00:25:12.260
in the company.

00:25:12.260 --> 00:25:13.243
They introduced Shiny.

00:25:13.243 --> 00:25:14.660
They suddenly built
an application

00:25:14.660 --> 00:25:16.100
that everyone likes.

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:18.010
And people just love
the application.

00:25:18.010 --> 00:25:20.210
The features are added
fast, because of the fact

00:25:20.210 --> 00:25:21.740
that Shiny is so robust.

00:25:21.740 --> 00:25:24.020
And the application
does exactly what people

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:26.230
need to in their daily job.

00:25:26.230 --> 00:25:28.190
And probably a lot
of you know this.

00:25:28.190 --> 00:25:30.620
Well, however, suddenly,
you have so many people

00:25:30.620 --> 00:25:31.990
using your application.

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:34.430
The application
starts getting slow.

00:25:34.430 --> 00:25:38.930
And this is usually when we jump
in, and help companies organize

00:25:38.930 --> 00:25:40.550
their code, and to
make sure that they

00:25:40.550 --> 00:25:43.440
can scale to a lot of people.

00:25:43.440 --> 00:25:45.990
Because the fact is
that Shiny's fast.

00:25:45.990 --> 00:25:47.510
RStudio Connect
is fast and allows

00:25:47.510 --> 00:25:51.080
you to scale even
up to 10,000 users.

00:25:51.080 --> 00:25:54.100
But it needs to be
implemented in a given way,

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:55.400
with a given architecture.

00:25:55.400 --> 00:25:58.520
And I'm going to share with you
what has to be done in order

00:25:58.520 --> 00:26:00.690
to achieve such scale.

00:26:00.690 --> 00:26:03.200
So first of all,
let's look at Shiny.

00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:05.670
There are two ways that you
can scale your application.

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:07.790
The first one is
vertical scaling.

00:26:07.790 --> 00:26:09.500
This is increasing
the amount of users

00:26:09.500 --> 00:26:11.180
that you have for one machine.

00:26:11.180 --> 00:26:13.070
And the other part is
horizontal scaling,

00:26:13.070 --> 00:26:14.960
which means adding
as many machines

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:18.830
as you want, just to make sure
that the application covers

00:26:18.830 --> 00:26:21.260
for as many people as you want.

00:26:21.260 --> 00:26:24.110
So one thing to know is
that you should first

00:26:24.110 --> 00:26:27.200
work on vertical scaling
so that your application is

00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:29.480
fast and very robust.

00:26:29.480 --> 00:26:31.920
And then you can add as
many machines as you want.

00:26:31.920 --> 00:26:35.140
Otherwise, you're going
to use a lot of resources.

00:26:35.140 --> 00:26:40.790
So for the first one,
there are three, basically,

00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:43.430
key things that you can do.

00:26:43.430 --> 00:26:45.440
First one is leverage
frontend, which

00:26:45.440 --> 00:26:48.580
is to use JavaScript to
handle fast user interactions.

00:26:48.580 --> 00:26:51.020
That do not change
the data and that's

00:26:51.020 --> 00:26:52.940
so that you then have
to actually communicate

00:26:52.940 --> 00:26:54.860
with the server that often.

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:57.170
The second part is to
extract computations

00:26:57.170 --> 00:26:59.900
to handle the resource
intensive operations away

00:26:59.900 --> 00:27:02.360
from the application.

00:27:02.360 --> 00:27:05.080
So that-- excuse me.

00:27:05.080 --> 00:27:10.950
Quick check-- so that
the server itself

00:27:10.950 --> 00:27:14.320
isn't that overloaded
with the computations.

00:27:14.320 --> 00:27:17.910
And the third one is to set
the right architecture that I'm

00:27:17.910 --> 00:27:19.537
going to show you as well.

00:27:19.537 --> 00:27:21.120
One thing that you
should keep in mind

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:24.130
is if you want your shiny
application to be fast,

00:27:24.130 --> 00:27:27.270
and if you want it to
work for many users,

00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:30.300
you should make sure that
the Shiny layer is thin.

00:27:30.300 --> 00:27:33.300
This should be a
communication interface

00:27:33.300 --> 00:27:36.150
between the data and the UI--

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:38.830
so the interface.

00:27:38.830 --> 00:27:40.290
So this is really
what we see when

00:27:40.290 --> 00:27:43.140
we join different companies
and work with them.

00:27:43.140 --> 00:27:43.920
There is a server.

00:27:43.920 --> 00:27:44.640
There is UI.

00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:46.260
And there is plenty
of communication

00:27:46.260 --> 00:27:49.650
happening between the
frontend and the backend.

00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:53.220
And what you should be doing
to make your application fast

00:27:53.220 --> 00:27:56.550
is first, you should make
sure that a lot of operations

00:27:56.550 --> 00:27:59.400
that do not need any
communication with server

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:01.260
happen on the front end.

00:28:01.260 --> 00:28:05.100
And that the server actually
calls the external services

00:28:05.100 --> 00:28:08.190
to fetch the data and to
filter the data there,

00:28:08.190 --> 00:28:11.520
not inside of the
memory of your machine.

00:28:11.520 --> 00:28:14.910
And then you can add as
many machines as you want.

00:28:14.910 --> 00:28:16.650
So let's take a look
at the first one--

00:28:16.650 --> 00:28:18.110
leveraging the frontend.

00:28:18.110 --> 00:28:19.620
Few very simple
things that you can

00:28:19.620 --> 00:28:23.160
do to already scale
your application.

00:28:23.160 --> 00:28:25.980
I'm going to go through
them very briefly.

00:28:25.980 --> 00:28:29.430
First one, render inputs
in UI and only update

00:28:29.430 --> 00:28:31.770
them in server.R. This
is a very simple thing

00:28:31.770 --> 00:28:34.260
that you can already apply
to your applications.

00:28:34.260 --> 00:28:38.070
Very often people create
a UI, outputs in the UI,

00:28:38.070 --> 00:28:41.670
and then they put all of their
rendering in the backend.

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:44.310
This requires rerendering
the whole widget

00:28:44.310 --> 00:28:45.830
whenever the inputs--

00:28:45.830 --> 00:28:48.120
one of the other inputs change.

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:50.340
And this makes the
application slower.

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:53.190
What instead you can do is you
can render the numeric input

00:28:53.190 --> 00:28:57.630
on the front end and only
update the inputs on the server.

00:28:57.630 --> 00:28:58.620
Very simple change.

00:28:58.620 --> 00:29:01.200
Already gives you
a lot of leverage.

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:04.020
The second part you can
inline JavaScript code

00:29:04.020 --> 00:29:07.320
with (shinyjs) package
by Dean Attali.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:10.440
Very simple thing, allows
you to call JavaScript right

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:11.580
from the server.

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:15.140
And you don't have to generate
the whole HTML on the backend,

00:29:15.140 --> 00:29:17.430
but you can just ask
JavaScript to toggle

00:29:17.430 --> 00:29:22.290
some changes in the UI without
actually going back and forth.

00:29:22.290 --> 00:29:25.470
And the third one is to set
all actions in JavaScript

00:29:25.470 --> 00:29:27.340
without server.R part.

00:29:27.340 --> 00:29:29.670
So when you create, for
example, an action button,

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:32.390
you can right away
create an on-click hook

00:29:32.390 --> 00:29:34.790
for the JavaScript call.

00:29:34.790 --> 00:29:37.430
And what is important
here is that if you

00:29:37.430 --> 00:29:39.950
have skilled people
in your team,

00:29:39.950 --> 00:29:42.400
and you have created a
great application that

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:45.470
is used by a lot of people,
and you want your team

00:29:45.470 --> 00:29:49.760
members to grow, the JavaScript
is a natural next step for them

00:29:49.760 --> 00:29:52.130
to learn to make the
applications robust,

00:29:52.130 --> 00:29:54.770
look better, and move faster.

00:29:54.770 --> 00:29:57.440
Let's talk about
extracting computations.

00:29:57.440 --> 00:30:00.050
First idea is to
use a remote API.

00:30:00.050 --> 00:30:03.330
And Plumber from RStudio is
a great package to do that.

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:06.120
So when you think
about your application,

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:09.500
you shouldn't load the entire
data set to your application.

00:30:09.500 --> 00:30:12.530
Instead you can ask
external services

00:30:12.530 --> 00:30:14.630
to filter the data
for you, and you just

00:30:14.630 --> 00:30:17.960
show the user the excerpt
of the data that is useful.

00:30:17.960 --> 00:30:20.760
Because very often,
as Pedro mentioned,

00:30:20.760 --> 00:30:23.690
you don't need to put too
much information to the user.

00:30:23.690 --> 00:30:26.240
They just want to
see what they need.

00:30:26.240 --> 00:30:29.060
Then you can very easily
grab data extraction logic

00:30:29.060 --> 00:30:30.670
into a simple API.

00:30:30.670 --> 00:30:34.940
With Plumber, this is done just
by adding special comments.

00:30:34.940 --> 00:30:37.250
There is a great
documentation about it.

00:30:37.250 --> 00:30:40.580
So make sure that you check it
out if you don't know Plumber.

00:30:40.580 --> 00:30:43.400
And what is really important
is that you can easily deploy

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:45.290
this with RStudio Connect.

00:30:45.290 --> 00:30:47.510
So just as you have your
applications running

00:30:47.510 --> 00:30:49.710
on RStudio Connect,
with a click of a button

00:30:49.710 --> 00:30:51.260
you can also deploy API.

00:30:51.260 --> 00:30:53.060
And many applications
can actually

00:30:53.060 --> 00:30:56.780
use the same API, which
allows you to reuse your code

00:30:56.780 --> 00:31:00.010
across many different projects.

00:31:00.010 --> 00:31:03.820
Side note-- make sure to use
efficient data libraries.

00:31:03.820 --> 00:31:05.890
Because very often,
when you have an API,

00:31:05.890 --> 00:31:07.150
you want to share some data.

00:31:07.150 --> 00:31:11.148
And you very often, also
read it from the disk.

00:31:11.148 --> 00:31:12.940
Actually, there are
many different packages

00:31:12.940 --> 00:31:13.840
for reading data.

00:31:13.840 --> 00:31:16.480
And you can read a
huge amount of data

00:31:16.480 --> 00:31:20.600
without affecting the
memory of the system a lot.

00:31:20.600 --> 00:31:23.110
So make sure you check
out those packages

00:31:23.110 --> 00:31:26.170
and check how they actually
improve the performance.

00:31:26.170 --> 00:31:28.240
Sometimes, just
changing the library

00:31:28.240 --> 00:31:30.760
that you use for
manipulating data

00:31:30.760 --> 00:31:34.830
can unblock a lot of
potential in your application.

00:31:34.830 --> 00:31:36.890
So for extracting
computations, the second part

00:31:36.890 --> 00:31:40.730
of this, very
often important and

00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:45.770
crucial for the applications
is to start using a database.

00:31:45.770 --> 00:31:47.235
To set the story,
we usually starts

00:31:47.235 --> 00:31:48.860
with a simple
application, and the data

00:31:48.860 --> 00:31:50.840
is just loaded into memory.

00:31:50.840 --> 00:31:51.350
Looks fine.

00:31:51.350 --> 00:31:51.965
I have a UI.

00:31:51.965 --> 00:31:52.870
I have the data.

00:31:52.870 --> 00:31:55.790
And then I have server
that filters the data

00:31:55.790 --> 00:31:58.340
and shows the
results to the user.

00:31:58.340 --> 00:31:59.760
Just a simple application.

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:03.050
However, if the file is big,
like for example, one gigabyte,

00:32:03.050 --> 00:32:05.600
it's going to cause
you some trouble.

00:32:05.600 --> 00:32:08.098
First, when you have
just five users,

00:32:08.098 --> 00:32:09.890
RStudio Connect is
going to distribute them

00:32:09.890 --> 00:32:12.385
evenly between processes.

00:32:12.385 --> 00:32:13.760
And so they are
not going to use,

00:32:13.760 --> 00:32:16.250
for example, three
gigabytes of RAM.

00:32:16.250 --> 00:32:19.060
If you have 13 users, it kind
of gets a little bit more,

00:32:19.060 --> 00:32:21.560
but the machine is
going to handle it well.

00:32:21.560 --> 00:32:24.740
However, the more users use
your application, the more

00:32:24.740 --> 00:32:26.390
processes you have to create.

00:32:26.390 --> 00:32:29.210
And each process is going
to keep the same object

00:32:29.210 --> 00:32:30.350
in the memory.

00:32:30.350 --> 00:32:32.360
So it is crucial
for you to instead

00:32:32.360 --> 00:32:36.710
of putting all of the data
into memory, to put it away

00:32:36.710 --> 00:32:39.710
to a database, and
to just use filter

00:32:39.710 --> 00:32:44.000
methods and different selections
to get the data that you need.

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:47.630
And then your machine is going
to be very light, very thin,

00:32:47.630 --> 00:32:50.090
and you can have a
huge amount of users

00:32:50.090 --> 00:32:51.050
using your application.

00:32:51.050 --> 00:32:54.500
And the database is going
to handle all of them.

00:32:54.500 --> 00:32:56.300
Also, important thing
to know is that you

00:32:56.300 --> 00:32:59.360
can use [INAUDIBLE] to
use different filtering,

00:32:59.360 --> 00:33:03.090
just as you are manipulating
data with [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:03.090 --> 00:33:05.180
So let's talk about
the architecture.

00:33:05.180 --> 00:33:06.770
We all know Shiny
Server Open Source.

00:33:06.770 --> 00:33:10.760
It allows us to very quickly
deploy the application.

00:33:10.760 --> 00:33:13.170
Right away, after you
deploy the application,

00:33:13.170 --> 00:33:15.920
you will see that RStudio
Connect to is a great choice

00:33:15.920 --> 00:33:20.360
to have multiple processes
to have better control on how

00:33:20.360 --> 00:33:21.830
users use your application.

00:33:24.500 --> 00:33:26.270
It also has a
great functionality

00:33:26.270 --> 00:33:28.460
of being able to
deploy applications

00:33:28.460 --> 00:33:30.260
with a single click of a button.

00:33:30.260 --> 00:33:32.450
What we like to
use on our site is

00:33:32.450 --> 00:33:36.720
we use Ansible to provision
the whole infrastructure.

00:33:36.720 --> 00:33:39.920
So imagine you have
a baremetal machine.

00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:42.170
With Ansible, you can install
all of the packages that

00:33:42.170 --> 00:33:43.462
are needed for RStudio Connect.

00:33:43.462 --> 00:33:45.185
Then you can install
RStudio Connect.

00:33:45.185 --> 00:33:47.060
And then you can even
deploy the application,

00:33:47.060 --> 00:33:50.240
because you have a great
package, RS Connect, which

00:33:50.240 --> 00:33:53.610
has the whole API to deploy
applications from the command

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:54.650
line.

00:33:54.650 --> 00:33:59.540
So really, I think that
everyone who does development

00:33:59.540 --> 00:34:02.460
on a high scale-- on a
big scale, using Ansible

00:34:02.460 --> 00:34:03.867
is a really good choice.

00:34:03.867 --> 00:34:05.450
Now, this is the
architecture that you

00:34:05.450 --> 00:34:08.570
would have for your applications
to set up multiple servers.

00:34:08.570 --> 00:34:10.850
And they all have to be
behind the load balancer

00:34:10.850 --> 00:34:12.199
with a sticky session.

00:34:12.199 --> 00:34:14.719
And they all have to connect
to the same database.

00:34:14.719 --> 00:34:18.620
They have the authentication
mechanism and the shared disk.

00:34:18.620 --> 00:34:22.060
As a bonus, there is also a
great option that you can use,

00:34:22.060 --> 00:34:24.320
so that, for
example, you can have

00:34:24.320 --> 00:34:26.360
offline mobile application.

00:34:26.360 --> 00:34:30.409
That is using the endpoints
that are deployed to actually

00:34:30.409 --> 00:34:31.400
connect with Plumber.

00:34:31.400 --> 00:34:34.190
So this is also something that
we have done successfully.

00:34:34.190 --> 00:34:36.889
So there is plenty of
things that you can do.

00:34:36.889 --> 00:34:39.350
And I encourage you to try them.

00:34:39.350 --> 00:34:41.550
You may think that this is all.

00:34:41.550 --> 00:34:43.040
Of course, this is all from me.

00:34:43.040 --> 00:34:45.230
But when you think
about it, you know

00:34:45.230 --> 00:34:46.580
how to create a application.

00:34:46.580 --> 00:34:48.590
You know how to
make it beautiful.

00:34:48.590 --> 00:34:50.510
You know how to
collaborate with your team.

00:34:50.510 --> 00:34:52.159
And then you can even scale it.

00:34:52.159 --> 00:34:53.750
But now comes the big thing.

00:34:53.750 --> 00:34:56.810
Because when you
have such success,

00:34:56.810 --> 00:34:59.300
then you want to share
this success with others.

00:34:59.300 --> 00:35:01.550
And that is how--

00:35:01.550 --> 00:35:03.470
that is why Alex is
going to tell you

00:35:03.470 --> 00:35:06.710
what you can do to actually
go beyond one single project

00:35:06.710 --> 00:35:08.240
and actually make
this collaboration

00:35:08.240 --> 00:35:10.440
fruitful for everyone.

00:35:10.440 --> 00:35:12.613
Thank you.

00:35:12.613 --> 00:35:13.530
ALEX K. GOLD: Awesome.

00:35:13.530 --> 00:35:15.570
Thanks, Damian.

00:35:15.570 --> 00:35:18.930
I will share my screen here
now, and start presenting.

00:35:18.930 --> 00:35:19.980
Great.

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:20.940
Hey, everybody.

00:35:20.940 --> 00:35:24.090
Thanks so much to all of my
co-presenters, Damian and Pedro

00:35:24.090 --> 00:35:28.320
and Olga for like leading
us through the better

00:35:28.320 --> 00:35:29.070
part of this hour.

00:35:29.070 --> 00:35:31.440
I'm super excited to sort
of close out here and talk

00:35:31.440 --> 00:35:33.140
a little bit about--

00:35:33.140 --> 00:35:34.710
let me try, how do I click?

00:35:34.710 --> 00:35:38.155
There we go-- about growing
a remote data science team.

00:35:38.155 --> 00:35:39.030
My name is Alex Gold.

00:35:39.030 --> 00:35:41.160
I'm a solutions
engineer at RStudio.

00:35:41.160 --> 00:35:42.660
And if you feel
like tweeting at me,

00:35:42.660 --> 00:35:45.180
my Twitter is down
there on the bottom.

00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:47.930
So let's jump right in-- growing
a remote data science team.

00:35:47.930 --> 00:35:49.388
Just to tell you
a little about me,

00:35:49.388 --> 00:35:52.110
this is my face, since you can't
see it on the video right now.

00:35:52.110 --> 00:35:54.175
More importantly,
this is my puppy.

00:35:54.175 --> 00:35:55.300
She's much cuter than I am.

00:35:57.910 --> 00:36:00.300
And so my background is
before I came to RStudio,

00:36:00.300 --> 00:36:04.290
I was a data scientist and
a data science team lead.

00:36:04.290 --> 00:36:06.470
Now here at RStudio, I work
as a solutions engineer.

00:36:06.470 --> 00:36:09.722
And what that means is that
I spend my time helping teams

00:36:09.722 --> 00:36:12.180
figure out how to make the most
of open source data science

00:36:12.180 --> 00:36:15.090
tools like R and Python, and
how to make the most of RStudio

00:36:15.090 --> 00:36:16.655
professional tools.

00:36:16.655 --> 00:36:18.030
So what I'm talking
about here is

00:36:18.030 --> 00:36:20.700
sort of a combination of
things that I learned as a data

00:36:20.700 --> 00:36:22.650
science team lead
and also some things

00:36:22.650 --> 00:36:25.140
I've seen out in the
wild throughout my time

00:36:25.140 --> 00:36:26.970
here at RStudio.

00:36:26.970 --> 00:36:30.360
So let's start by talking a
little about like what it means

00:36:30.360 --> 00:36:32.550
to grow a data science team.

00:36:32.550 --> 00:36:34.000
And so there are
really four ways

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:35.750
I think about growing
a data science team.

00:36:35.750 --> 00:36:37.210
The first is just
straight-up size.

00:36:37.210 --> 00:36:40.410
Like the number of
people on the team

00:36:40.410 --> 00:36:42.270
is one way to grow
a data science team.

00:36:42.270 --> 00:36:44.860
You can also grow a data science
team in terms of velocity.

00:36:44.860 --> 00:36:46.330
Like what is the
output per person?

00:36:46.330 --> 00:36:47.580
How much Are you churning out?

00:36:47.580 --> 00:36:48.870
How much work are
you getting done?

00:36:48.870 --> 00:36:50.245
How many models
are you creating?

00:36:50.245 --> 00:36:51.762
That sort of thing.

00:36:51.762 --> 00:36:53.970
Another way you can sort of
scale a data science team

00:36:53.970 --> 00:36:55.273
is in terms of reliability.

00:36:55.273 --> 00:36:57.690
Are you confident that the
outputs that you're putting out

00:36:57.690 --> 00:36:59.250
are correct?

00:36:59.250 --> 00:37:02.370
Are you confident that
they're what they need to be?

00:37:02.370 --> 00:37:03.870
And then, of course,
sophistication.

00:37:03.870 --> 00:37:06.150
And that could mean
maybe graduating

00:37:06.150 --> 00:37:08.070
to using more advanced
machine learning

00:37:08.070 --> 00:37:09.330
models or things like that.

00:37:09.330 --> 00:37:12.810
Or maybe it just means
going from using mostly

00:37:12.810 --> 00:37:14.310
Excel to using more code tools.

00:37:14.310 --> 00:37:17.430
That can be a really
great way to grow

00:37:17.430 --> 00:37:19.270
a team in particular way.

00:37:19.270 --> 00:37:22.350
So what I'm going to spend
the balance of my time talking

00:37:22.350 --> 00:37:25.680
about today is actually a
tool that I think can really

00:37:25.680 --> 00:37:27.680
help with all of these things.

00:37:27.680 --> 00:37:30.150
And that tool of course
is the R package.

00:37:30.150 --> 00:37:31.900
Some of you might be
thinking, like, wait.

00:37:31.900 --> 00:37:32.380
What?

00:37:32.380 --> 00:37:33.470
You're just going to
talk about R packages?

00:37:33.470 --> 00:37:35.053
But I want to take
a little step back.

00:37:35.053 --> 00:37:37.692
So instead of talking about
the mechanics of R packages,

00:37:37.692 --> 00:37:39.150
like how do you
write in R package.

00:37:39.150 --> 00:37:39.870
I'm not going to
talk about that.

00:37:39.870 --> 00:37:42.030
There's lots of great
stuff written about it.

00:37:42.030 --> 00:37:43.980
I really recommend like
Hadley Wickham's book,

00:37:43.980 --> 00:37:45.220
if you're curious.

00:37:45.220 --> 00:37:47.310
That's a great resource.

00:37:47.310 --> 00:37:50.820
But what I want to talk about
is the R package as a tool

00:37:50.820 --> 00:37:52.680
to grow a data science team.

00:37:52.680 --> 00:37:55.700
How does the R package work for
us, not what is the R package.

00:37:55.700 --> 00:37:57.450
But let's start off
just like describing--

00:37:57.450 --> 00:37:59.640
for anybody who's not
super familiar-- like what

00:37:59.640 --> 00:38:02.730
is in R package.

00:38:02.730 --> 00:38:05.340
So R package is a way
to sort of container

00:38:05.340 --> 00:38:09.180
up package up, a bunch of
functions that you use commonly

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:12.690
in R. Like much like you could
put a dog in a cardboard box,

00:38:12.690 --> 00:38:14.400
and it would be
nicely contained.

00:38:14.400 --> 00:38:17.850
A package is a way to contain
a bunch of R functions

00:38:17.850 --> 00:38:20.590
that sort of hang
together for some reason.

00:38:20.590 --> 00:38:23.220
And so I want to talk
about two different ways

00:38:23.220 --> 00:38:28.000
to use R packages on your data
science team to help it grow.

00:38:28.000 --> 00:38:31.710
And so the first way is
the teamwide R package.

00:38:31.710 --> 00:38:35.340
So this is when your team
writes one-- or sometimes a few,

00:38:35.340 --> 00:38:37.590
sometimes the team will have
several depending on what

00:38:37.590 --> 00:38:38.370
you're doing--

00:38:38.370 --> 00:38:41.850
R packages that are
for specific things.

00:38:41.850 --> 00:38:43.400
And they're used
across the team.

00:38:43.400 --> 00:38:45.870
So the kinds of things you
might-- like having those R

00:38:45.870 --> 00:38:46.710
packages.

00:38:46.710 --> 00:38:48.870
Maybe you have particular
analytical functions

00:38:48.870 --> 00:38:50.790
that you use certain,
machine-learning models.

00:38:50.790 --> 00:38:53.070
Maybe you always
weight your regression

00:38:53.070 --> 00:38:55.360
the same way or
something like that.

00:38:55.360 --> 00:38:56.610
And that can go--

00:38:56.610 --> 00:38:58.410
those functions go
in an R package.

00:38:58.410 --> 00:39:00.485
Maybe a certain plotting
default you use a lot.

00:39:00.485 --> 00:39:01.860
Maybe you use the
same templates.

00:39:01.860 --> 00:39:04.320
Or maybe you actually have
the same types of plots.

00:39:04.320 --> 00:39:05.737
You're creating a
lot, and putting

00:39:05.737 --> 00:39:08.908
those plotting functions into
R package can really help.

00:39:08.908 --> 00:39:09.950
Maybe you have templates.

00:39:09.950 --> 00:39:11.200
You can put code
templates in R package.

00:39:11.200 --> 00:39:12.820
You can also put R
Markdown templates

00:39:12.820 --> 00:39:15.912
in R package, which I think is
a really cool use of R package.

00:39:15.912 --> 00:39:17.370
You can have a
report template that

00:39:17.370 --> 00:39:19.328
is the same way that you
use every time, put it

00:39:19.328 --> 00:39:22.330
in a package, now, everybody
across the team has it.

00:39:22.330 --> 00:39:25.170
And then the last
part of things that

00:39:25.170 --> 00:39:27.000
are really great in
teamwide R packages

00:39:27.000 --> 00:39:28.920
are data accessing functions.

00:39:28.920 --> 00:39:33.060
Maybe you have particular ways
you use the DBI package or ODBC

00:39:33.060 --> 00:39:34.860
and wrapping that
into an R package

00:39:34.860 --> 00:39:37.990
means that not everybody has
to remember how it works.

00:39:37.990 --> 00:39:39.990
They can just use the
function in the R package.

00:39:39.990 --> 00:39:41.160
And that's really handy.

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:44.065
So this is what goes in
the Team-Wide R package.

00:39:44.065 --> 00:39:45.690
But as I said, we're
not going to focus

00:39:45.690 --> 00:39:46.732
so much on the mechanics.

00:39:46.732 --> 00:39:48.800
What then is this R package for?

00:39:48.800 --> 00:39:52.570
And to me, a team-wide R package
is really for three purposes.

00:39:52.570 --> 00:39:54.360
The first is size.

00:39:54.360 --> 00:39:56.610
It's for scaling your
team, particularly

00:39:56.610 --> 00:39:58.303
now that we're remote.

00:39:58.303 --> 00:40:00.720
You can't sit next to somebody
and talk them through like,

00:40:00.720 --> 00:40:02.610
this is how we do an analysis.

00:40:02.610 --> 00:40:05.910
And so the documentation and
the functions in R package

00:40:05.910 --> 00:40:09.390
are a really great tool
to onboard new people

00:40:09.390 --> 00:40:11.557
to your team.

00:40:11.557 --> 00:40:13.140
And R packages are
a really great tool

00:40:13.140 --> 00:40:16.050
for increasing
velocity on your team.

00:40:16.050 --> 00:40:18.420
If you have functions
in there that

00:40:18.420 --> 00:40:20.790
make your work much
easier, and particularly,

00:40:20.790 --> 00:40:22.680
if you, with every
product, you go back

00:40:22.680 --> 00:40:24.540
and improve that R
package a little bit,

00:40:24.540 --> 00:40:26.525
that's going to
make the work just--

00:40:26.525 --> 00:40:27.900
every product is
going to get get

00:40:27.900 --> 00:40:31.260
easier and easier and easier as
that R package gets developed

00:40:31.260 --> 00:40:32.020
further.

00:40:32.020 --> 00:40:33.872
And of course, sophistication.

00:40:33.872 --> 00:40:35.580
If you have, say, one
person on your team

00:40:35.580 --> 00:40:37.038
that's just a really
great R coder,

00:40:37.038 --> 00:40:40.330
you can take advantage of their
skills across the entire team.

00:40:40.330 --> 00:40:43.410
Or if you like one really great
machine learning engineer.

00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:46.830
If they write those specialized
machine learning functions,

00:40:46.830 --> 00:40:49.020
everybody can use
them all of a sudden.

00:40:49.020 --> 00:40:51.720
To me the team-wide R
package is really a tool

00:40:51.720 --> 00:40:53.850
for onboarding and upscaling.

00:40:53.850 --> 00:40:56.500
That's the purpose of
the team-wide R package.

00:40:57.340 --> 00:40:59.160
I do want to give you
two keys to making

00:40:59.160 --> 00:41:00.740
this work for you, though.

00:41:00.740 --> 00:41:02.790
So the first key
is that everyone

00:41:02.790 --> 00:41:04.140
contributes to the package.

00:41:04.140 --> 00:41:06.270
By having everyone on
the team contribute,

00:41:06.270 --> 00:41:07.770
you get much more buy in.

00:41:07.770 --> 00:41:08.760
People are excited.

00:41:08.760 --> 00:41:09.960
They're excited to
use their own work.

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:11.585
They're excited to
use the team's work.

00:41:11.585 --> 00:41:13.950
And having that team write R
packages are really, really

00:41:13.950 --> 00:41:17.610
valuable resource, in that case,
that they will actually gets

00:41:17.610 --> 00:41:18.150
used.

00:41:18.150 --> 00:41:20.700
And the second key, which
is way more important,

00:41:20.700 --> 00:41:22.770
is that your team-wide
R package must

00:41:22.770 --> 00:41:24.780
have a clever name
using the capital

00:41:24.780 --> 00:41:26.760
letter R. This is not optional.

00:41:26.760 --> 00:41:27.990
It is required.

00:41:27.990 --> 00:41:29.430
But I will give you a hint.

00:41:29.430 --> 00:41:32.530
Blank blank helpR
is always available.

00:41:32.530 --> 00:41:36.870
So you have no excuse for
not having a clever name

00:41:36.870 --> 00:41:39.630
using the capital letter.

00:41:39.630 --> 00:41:42.180
So that's the pattern
of the team-wide R

00:41:42.180 --> 00:41:44.070
package or R packages.

00:41:44.070 --> 00:41:47.370
Another really popular pattern
is the package per project.

00:41:47.370 --> 00:41:51.360
That is like every analytical
project gets its own R package.

00:41:51.360 --> 00:41:54.240
And this is a really, really
great pattern as well.

00:41:54.240 --> 00:41:57.570
But to me, again, the
team-wide R package

00:41:57.570 --> 00:42:01.680
is about upscaling,
scaling, and upskilling.

00:42:01.680 --> 00:42:03.450
The package per
product is really

00:42:03.450 --> 00:42:05.413
about reliability,
that is that it

00:42:05.413 --> 00:42:07.080
allows you to document
what you're doing

00:42:07.080 --> 00:42:08.580
in a much more thorough way.

00:42:08.580 --> 00:42:10.710
And it allows you to
test what you're doing

00:42:10.710 --> 00:42:12.270
in a much more thorough way.

00:42:12.270 --> 00:42:14.620
When you put those
functions into a package,

00:42:14.620 --> 00:42:19.170
as opposed to just sort of
using them in your analysis

00:42:19.170 --> 00:42:21.280
and just sort of
having them that way.

00:42:21.280 --> 00:42:24.930
So I do want to go through this
in just a little more detail

00:42:24.930 --> 00:42:26.730
here, which is that--

00:42:26.730 --> 00:42:29.370
So this is how I would
structure a project

00:42:29.370 --> 00:42:32.880
that I were using this R
package per project structure.

00:42:32.880 --> 00:42:37.138
And so what I would here is
I have my top level project

00:42:37.138 --> 00:42:39.180
director, so that would
be like the whole project

00:42:39.180 --> 00:42:40.305
to be encapsulated in this.

00:42:40.305 --> 00:42:41.910
Incidentally, if I'm
using a Git repo,

00:42:41.910 --> 00:42:44.520
I would put it at the
level of the whole project.

00:42:44.520 --> 00:42:47.670
You can have sort of these
sub directories inside one git

00:42:47.670 --> 00:42:49.470
repo.

00:42:49.470 --> 00:42:52.030
Inside that though, I'd have
these two subdirectories out

00:42:52.030 --> 00:42:54.325
of the package and the project,
and have those separate

00:42:54.325 --> 00:42:56.950
from each other-- and then I'll
come back to that in a second--

00:42:56.950 --> 00:42:59.140
but they're sort of
parallel to each other.

00:42:59.140 --> 00:43:02.110
The package and the project
get developed in parallel.

00:43:02.110 --> 00:43:05.310
And something like dev tools
load_all is your friend here

00:43:05.310 --> 00:43:08.190
while you're while
you're working on this

00:43:08.190 --> 00:43:09.600
And then you have your--

00:43:09.600 --> 00:43:11.512
project functions go
inside your R directory.

00:43:11.512 --> 00:43:12.720
And this is where you write--

00:43:12.720 --> 00:43:14.512
you can put your
[INAUDIBLE] documentation.

00:43:14.512 --> 00:43:16.650
You can actually document
all of the functions

00:43:16.650 --> 00:43:18.420
you're using in your analysis.

00:43:18.420 --> 00:43:20.220
And that's a really
great thing to be

00:43:20.220 --> 00:43:24.048
able to have that documented
when you come back to it later.

00:43:24.048 --> 00:43:25.840
And of course, then
you can also use tests.

00:43:25.840 --> 00:43:29.340
And being able to unit test
your code is really important.

00:43:29.340 --> 00:43:31.620
And this is actually really
important beyond just sort

00:43:31.620 --> 00:43:34.320
of reliability that
you care about.

00:43:34.320 --> 00:43:36.165
So maybe you just
want to make sure

00:43:36.165 --> 00:43:38.040
your products more
reliable across the board,

00:43:38.040 --> 00:43:39.480
and that's a really great thing.

00:43:39.480 --> 00:43:41.250
But also in some
places, there are

00:43:41.250 --> 00:43:43.080
requirements that
everything be tested

00:43:43.080 --> 00:43:44.370
before it go to production.

00:43:44.370 --> 00:43:47.340
This is a really great way to
pull some of those tests out

00:43:47.340 --> 00:43:49.410
of an interactive mode
and put them somewhere

00:43:49.410 --> 00:43:50.960
that you can actually
run a script.

00:43:50.960 --> 00:43:52.290
And so this is one of
the really great things

00:43:52.290 --> 00:43:53.082
about the project--

00:43:53.082 --> 00:43:56.870
R package per project model.

00:43:56.870 --> 00:44:00.110
Then of course, you have your
actual whatever the actual work

00:44:00.110 --> 00:44:01.580
is of your project then.

00:44:01.580 --> 00:44:02.912
Sit separately from that right.

00:44:02.912 --> 00:44:05.120
That's in your-- you may
have an R Markdown document.

00:44:05.120 --> 00:44:07.490
You may have an app.R.
You have some dot R files.

00:44:07.490 --> 00:44:11.540
You might have a plumber API
like Damian talked about.

00:44:11.540 --> 00:44:13.430
All kinds of things can go here.

00:44:13.430 --> 00:44:14.977
But that's separate
from the package.

00:44:14.977 --> 00:44:17.060
And so I want to stick on
this point for a minute.

00:44:17.060 --> 00:44:19.340
If any of you are on RStudio--

00:44:19.340 --> 00:44:22.310
R stats Twitter, there's been
a really great discussion

00:44:22.310 --> 00:44:27.620
the last few days of should
each project be a package.

00:44:27.620 --> 00:44:30.350
Not like the project--

00:44:30.350 --> 00:44:32.300
the package per
project is-- should

00:44:32.300 --> 00:44:33.770
the project have a package.

00:44:33.770 --> 00:44:34.783
And I would say yes.

00:44:34.783 --> 00:44:36.950
There's also this whole
debate of should the project

00:44:36.950 --> 00:44:38.270
be a package.

00:44:38.270 --> 00:44:40.350
And my argument is no on that.

00:44:40.350 --> 00:44:42.740
To be clear, I spent the last
eight minutes just saying

00:44:42.740 --> 00:44:45.740
packages are amazing as a
tool for developing your team.

00:44:45.740 --> 00:44:48.218
But I don't think every
package should be a project.

00:44:48.218 --> 00:44:49.760
Mainly, because you
end up with a lot

00:44:49.760 --> 00:44:51.530
of weird metadata
on your project

00:44:51.530 --> 00:44:52.850
that doesn't work quite right.

00:44:52.850 --> 00:44:55.700
You can end up with some sort
of weird circular dependencies

00:44:55.700 --> 00:44:57.710
where the package
depends on kind of itself

00:44:57.710 --> 00:44:59.010
to do stuff in itself.

00:44:59.010 --> 00:45:00.703
And that's really messy.

00:45:00.703 --> 00:45:02.120
And also you can
end up in a state

00:45:02.120 --> 00:45:04.010
where your package isn't
actually installable.

00:45:04.010 --> 00:45:05.302
That's not a great place to be.

00:45:05.302 --> 00:45:08.690
So I would recommend the
package can be a great tool

00:45:08.690 --> 00:45:11.852
team-wide to share
functions across people.

00:45:11.852 --> 00:45:14.060
It can be a great tool for
increasing the reliability

00:45:14.060 --> 00:45:15.248
of an individual project.

00:45:15.248 --> 00:45:16.790
It's not meant to
be a whole project.

00:45:16.790 --> 00:45:18.200
But that's not the
intent of R package.

00:45:18.200 --> 00:45:19.610
And I would argue
at least-- and there

00:45:19.610 --> 00:45:22.193
are others who will argue very
intelligently, to the contrary.

00:45:22.193 --> 00:45:25.780
But I will argue that you should
not have your entire project be

00:45:25.780 --> 00:45:28.070
in R package.

00:45:28.070 --> 00:45:29.810
Thank you so much for tuning in.

00:45:29.810 --> 00:45:32.450
Just a couple-- my
high-level takeaways.

00:45:32.450 --> 00:45:36.140
R packages are a tool
for scaling and skilling

00:45:36.140 --> 00:45:37.952
your remote data science team.

00:45:37.952 --> 00:45:39.410
A team-wide package
is a great tool

00:45:39.410 --> 00:45:41.780
for increasing size,
velocity, ans sophistication.

00:45:41.780 --> 00:45:43.670
And a per project
package is a great tool

00:45:43.670 --> 00:45:47.180
for increasing the
reliability of your work.

00:45:47.180 --> 00:45:51.650
With that, I'm going to pull
back up this slide from Sam,

00:45:51.650 --> 00:45:57.308
and I'll hand it over
to her to close us out.

00:45:57.308 --> 00:45:58.163
SAM: All right.

00:45:58.163 --> 00:45:59.080
[INAUDIBLE] wonderful.

00:45:59.080 --> 00:46:00.520
Thank you so much, Alex.

00:46:00.520 --> 00:46:03.100
And thank you so much to
all of the presenters.

00:46:03.100 --> 00:46:05.980
I thought that was a really
fantastic story, starting

00:46:05.980 --> 00:46:08.830
from how to build a team, to
the actual tools you need,

00:46:08.830 --> 00:46:11.230
down to what to be successful.

00:46:11.230 --> 00:46:15.370
So let's go ahead and jump
into some questions here.

00:46:15.370 --> 00:46:19.390
I've got a few plugged in.

00:46:19.390 --> 00:46:20.980
We've got the first
one here saying,

00:46:20.980 --> 00:46:23.470
could you elaborate I think
this is going to be for Olga

00:46:23.470 --> 00:46:25.870
could you elaborate on
how people technically

00:46:25.870 --> 00:46:30.100
work together using
renv, Docker, and GitLab?

00:46:30.100 --> 00:46:34.945
What's the operational pipeline
here to use as a best practice?

00:46:34.945 --> 00:46:41.680
OLGA MIERZWA-SULIMA: So usually,
how we do that development is

00:46:41.680 --> 00:46:45.970
that when the project starts--

00:46:45.970 --> 00:46:52.690
so at the beginning we built
a dedicated Docker container

00:46:52.690 --> 00:46:54.400
for each project.

00:46:54.400 --> 00:47:01.720
And inside the Docker container,
we set up an R studio.

00:47:01.720 --> 00:47:05.050
So we do the development
inside the Docker container.

00:47:05.050 --> 00:47:11.980
And to keep track of all of the
packages and their versions,

00:47:11.980 --> 00:47:15.220
and to make the collaboration
for the users easy,

00:47:15.220 --> 00:47:18.910
we use renv package,
which is the package

00:47:18.910 --> 00:47:22.240
available from RStudio.

00:47:22.240 --> 00:47:24.280
And whenever there is--

00:47:24.280 --> 00:47:26.710
whenever we install
a new package,

00:47:26.710 --> 00:47:33.520
we install it to renv so the
renv lockfile is updated.

00:47:33.520 --> 00:47:38.420
And later, when we
finish the work,

00:47:38.420 --> 00:47:43.660
we need to do the-- either we
do-- we comment the changes.

00:47:43.660 --> 00:47:48.490
So we comment in both the
Docker container and the changes

00:47:48.490 --> 00:47:49.420
to the codes.

00:47:49.420 --> 00:47:52.720
And we can either
[? recommit ?] them.

00:47:52.720 --> 00:47:56.050
If we used GitHub, then we
do it in GitHub or GitLab.

00:47:56.050 --> 00:48:02.340
It doesn't really matter
which service you use.

00:48:02.340 --> 00:48:09.160
And the Docker file--

00:48:09.160 --> 00:48:12.530
we use [INAUDIBLE] or
you can also rebuild.

00:48:12.530 --> 00:48:16.900
The Docker image is a push
to the Docker Hub repository.

00:48:16.900 --> 00:48:21.280
So basically, this
is our work flow.

00:48:21.280 --> 00:48:26.590
And how to exactly
setup renv with Docker,

00:48:26.590 --> 00:48:32.020
there is a vignette available
from renv package that

00:48:32.020 --> 00:48:37.070
more or less describes
how to do this.

00:48:37.070 --> 00:48:38.630
PEDRO SILVA: Just
want to quickly add

00:48:38.630 --> 00:48:43.340
something, very often we
also have a base image that

00:48:43.340 --> 00:48:45.500
already contains
the cached libraries

00:48:45.500 --> 00:48:47.240
that you very often use.

00:48:47.240 --> 00:48:52.727
And multiple projects can
use this image as a base.

00:48:52.727 --> 00:48:54.560
So that you don't have
to install everything

00:48:54.560 --> 00:48:55.420
from scratch.

00:48:55.420 --> 00:48:57.170
So that's also something
that we recommend

00:48:57.170 --> 00:48:58.628
if you want to have
reproducibility

00:48:58.628 --> 00:49:00.056
across your team.

00:49:00.056 --> 00:49:00.960
SAM: That's great.

00:49:00.960 --> 00:49:01.950
Thank you so much.

00:49:01.950 --> 00:49:05.775
And in that, Olga are you
using RStudio desktop,

00:49:05.775 --> 00:49:08.970
or RStudio Server,
RStudio Server pro,

00:49:08.970 --> 00:49:11.192
what does your tooling
look like there?

00:49:11.192 --> 00:49:14.710
OLGA MIERZWA-SULIMA:
Well, this really depends.

00:49:14.710 --> 00:49:19.420
So either we are using
the RStudio desktop

00:49:19.420 --> 00:49:26.710
or also, we can simplify
this workflow as well.

00:49:26.710 --> 00:49:30.160
So get rid of the Docker.

00:49:30.160 --> 00:49:32.350
But then, we are
obviously working

00:49:32.350 --> 00:49:37.750
with RStudio Server pro.

00:49:37.750 --> 00:49:41.020
But then, we still
use renv to take

00:49:41.020 --> 00:49:47.160
care of the control
over the packages.

00:49:47.160 --> 00:49:53.530
But it really depends from
what actually our clients

00:49:53.530 --> 00:49:54.310
want to achieve.

00:49:59.070 --> 00:50:00.288
SAM: Let's see.

00:50:00.288 --> 00:50:00.830
That's great.

00:50:00.830 --> 00:50:01.790
Thank you.

00:50:01.790 --> 00:50:02.760
And then, let's see.

00:50:02.760 --> 00:50:06.170
I'm just looking at making sure
we got questions for everyone.

00:50:06.170 --> 00:50:06.890
I see.

00:50:06.890 --> 00:50:10.670
Here, I believe this
one is for Damian.

00:50:10.670 --> 00:50:12.860
"So what recommendations
can you make

00:50:12.860 --> 00:50:15.860
when the dashboard requires
permissions and content

00:50:15.860 --> 00:50:19.690
dependent on user
viewing the dashboard?"

00:50:19.690 --> 00:50:22.330
Follow up on that, is
LDAP a good fit here,

00:50:22.330 --> 00:50:25.330
or what other LDAP or other
authentication sources

00:50:25.330 --> 00:50:27.421
might you use?

00:50:27.421 --> 00:50:30.410
DAMIAN RODZIEWICZ:
So I find it to be--

00:50:30.410 --> 00:50:35.360
there are two ways of handling
the access to the data.

00:50:35.360 --> 00:50:37.290
One part of course
is authentication.

00:50:37.290 --> 00:50:40.460
So who is allowed to
access the application?

00:50:40.460 --> 00:50:42.110
And two, with
RStudio Connect, you

00:50:42.110 --> 00:50:45.380
can actually hook
up to any service

00:50:45.380 --> 00:50:47.810
that you have internally
in your company.

00:50:47.810 --> 00:50:50.030
Very often, companies
use LDAP to have

00:50:50.030 --> 00:50:51.680
the same users,
the same passwords,

00:50:51.680 --> 00:50:53.920
and you can also have
a single sign on,

00:50:53.920 --> 00:50:58.220
and all the other good things
with our RStudio Connect.

00:50:58.220 --> 00:51:01.190
When it comes to
authorization, it

00:51:01.190 --> 00:51:03.032
means that you can
have two users logged

00:51:03.032 --> 00:51:04.490
into the same
application, but they

00:51:04.490 --> 00:51:06.645
see different parts of the app.

00:51:06.645 --> 00:51:08.270
And usually, this is
something that you

00:51:08.270 --> 00:51:10.850
have to implement yourself
within the application

00:51:10.850 --> 00:51:14.420
by assigning roles within the
app with the business context

00:51:14.420 --> 00:51:15.505
that you have.

00:51:15.505 --> 00:51:17.130
ALEX K. GOLD: One
quick thing I'll just

00:51:17.130 --> 00:51:19.320
add to what Damian said,
which is exactly right,

00:51:19.320 --> 00:51:22.480
is that one of the ways you
can do that inside connect

00:51:22.480 --> 00:51:24.630
is that you get in
the session data.

00:51:24.630 --> 00:51:26.160
You get the user and the group.

00:51:26.160 --> 00:51:27.577
So that's one of
the ways that you

00:51:27.577 --> 00:51:30.000
can take that data
in a running session

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:32.400
and then pass it along to--

00:51:32.400 --> 00:51:35.100
use it to do authorization,
is that you actually get

00:51:35.100 --> 00:51:38.255
that data inside the session.

00:51:38.255 --> 00:51:39.380
DAMIAN RODZIEWICZ: Exactly.

00:51:39.380 --> 00:51:40.670
That's a very good point.

00:51:45.906 --> 00:51:46.900
SAM: That's great.

00:51:46.900 --> 00:51:47.680
Thank you.

00:51:47.680 --> 00:51:51.522
So now let's quickly jump
to some package talk.

00:51:51.522 --> 00:51:52.730
I'm sure everyone loves that.

00:51:52.730 --> 00:51:54.730
So Alex, what are
your recommendations

00:51:54.730 --> 00:51:56.650
when functionality
should be extracted

00:51:56.650 --> 00:52:00.940
from a project-specific
package to a team-wide package?

00:52:00.940 --> 00:52:04.270
ALEX K. GOLD: Oh,
that's a great question.

00:52:04.270 --> 00:52:08.560
My favorite reference
here is David Robinson

00:52:08.560 --> 00:52:11.450
who's a data scientist and sort
of big in the stats community.

00:52:11.450 --> 00:52:12.690
He has a very--

00:52:12.690 --> 00:52:17.060
he says and repeats that
you should do a lot of work

00:52:17.060 --> 00:52:17.560
in public.

00:52:17.560 --> 00:52:20.500
And if you say something
three times to somebody,

00:52:20.500 --> 00:52:23.460
you should put it
in a blog post.

00:52:23.460 --> 00:52:25.060
And my sort of
corollary to that is

00:52:25.060 --> 00:52:28.488
that if you find yourself using
the same function three times,

00:52:28.488 --> 00:52:29.780
you should put it in a package.

00:52:29.780 --> 00:52:33.130
So that would be
my recommendation.

00:52:33.130 --> 00:52:35.320
Certainly, if you have
three projects where you're

00:52:35.320 --> 00:52:37.153
using the same function,
you should probably

00:52:37.153 --> 00:52:39.490
extract that into a package.

00:52:39.490 --> 00:52:42.210
I would say sometimes
even two is worth it.

00:52:42.210 --> 00:52:44.740
But to me, really the flag
is like, do I want to use

00:52:44.740 --> 00:52:48.410
this package, use this function
more than once or twice?

00:52:48.410 --> 00:52:50.680
Then it belongs in sort
of a team-wide package.

00:52:50.680 --> 00:52:53.195
For the per project
package pattern,

00:52:53.195 --> 00:52:54.820
you don't necessarily
need to reuse it.

00:52:54.820 --> 00:52:57.040
Sometimes the testing
and the documentation

00:52:57.040 --> 00:53:00.290
is worth extracting into
that package all by itself.

00:53:00.290 --> 00:53:01.750
But if you're thinking about--

00:53:01.750 --> 00:53:04.083
obviously you want to keep
the namespace of your package

00:53:04.083 --> 00:53:04.930
somewhat compact.

00:53:04.930 --> 00:53:09.520
And so waiting until you need
it two or three times to pull it

00:53:09.520 --> 00:53:12.844
in is often worthwhile.

00:53:12.844 --> 00:53:15.740
DAMIAN RODZIEWICZ:
If I may add to this,

00:53:15.740 --> 00:53:18.580
the greater tool that
you can use a product

00:53:18.580 --> 00:53:21.700
is RStudio Package
Manager, which is basically

00:53:21.700 --> 00:53:25.650
a copy of CRAN within your own
organization that you control.

00:53:25.650 --> 00:53:27.830
And you can install all
of the packages there.

00:53:27.830 --> 00:53:31.090
And this allows you to share
this one package, which

00:53:31.090 --> 00:53:33.220
is private to your
organization, to everyone

00:53:33.220 --> 00:53:34.695
within the organization.

00:53:34.695 --> 00:53:35.820
So be sure to check it out.

00:53:39.915 --> 00:53:40.850
SAM: That's great.

00:53:40.850 --> 00:53:41.630
Thank you, Damian.

00:53:41.630 --> 00:53:45.320
So I think we've got enough
time for one last question here.

00:53:45.320 --> 00:53:48.957
I think this is a question
that any anybody could answer.

00:53:48.957 --> 00:53:51.290
So-- and I'd love to hear
what all different folks think

00:53:51.290 --> 00:53:51.800
about that.

00:53:51.800 --> 00:53:55.070
So what are some strategies
to maintain consistency

00:53:55.070 --> 00:53:59.060
in file locations on the
server for data: databases,

00:53:59.060 --> 00:54:02.960
R scripts, R Markdown
projects, and dashboards?

00:54:02.960 --> 00:54:05.630
We have issues with files
everywhere but no structure.

00:54:05.630 --> 00:54:07.130
So what are some
tips that you might

00:54:07.130 --> 00:54:09.260
have on maintaining
that consistency?

00:54:13.157 --> 00:54:14.990
ALEX K. GOLD: I'll go
ahead just real quick.

00:54:14.990 --> 00:54:17.650
So I think a really
great solution for this

00:54:17.650 --> 00:54:21.460
is to use a
project-based workflow.

00:54:21.460 --> 00:54:23.710
If you look up the
Tidyverse blog,

00:54:23.710 --> 00:54:26.260
Jenny Bryan has a
great article on using

00:54:26.260 --> 00:54:28.035
a project-based workflow.

00:54:28.035 --> 00:54:29.410
And so what this
allows you to do

00:54:29.410 --> 00:54:32.200
is to sort of make projects
more self-contained.

00:54:32.200 --> 00:54:34.400
I think that's a really
great place to start.

00:54:34.400 --> 00:54:37.930
Obviously there are other tools
in terms of consolidating data,

00:54:37.930 --> 00:54:40.540
and database access,
and things like that,

00:54:40.540 --> 00:54:42.550
but starting with a
project-based workflow,

00:54:42.550 --> 00:54:45.850
I think, is a really powerful
first step to making things

00:54:45.850 --> 00:54:48.577
sort of-- more projects and
work more self-contained.

00:54:48.577 --> 00:54:49.660
That and a package, right?

00:54:49.660 --> 00:54:52.240
A project and a package
go together really nicely

00:54:52.240 --> 00:54:56.332
for keeping things
self-contained.

00:54:56.332 --> 00:54:58.170
DAMIAN RODZIEWICZ: The
points from my side

00:54:58.170 --> 00:55:01.380
that I would add,
if you want to have

00:55:01.380 --> 00:55:07.170
a great, clean code within
just one application,

00:55:07.170 --> 00:55:09.730
I'd recommend learning
about different designs.

00:55:09.730 --> 00:55:11.850
For example,
domain-driven design

00:55:11.850 --> 00:55:15.900
is a great way of
structuring projects.

00:55:15.900 --> 00:55:18.543
And it applies to many
different languages.

00:55:18.543 --> 00:55:19.960
There is plenty
of books about it.

00:55:19.960 --> 00:55:21.710
And they actually give
you a good overview

00:55:21.710 --> 00:55:24.570
on how you can build your
functions, your files,

00:55:24.570 --> 00:55:27.280
so that they are
understandable to everyone.

00:55:27.280 --> 00:55:31.420
And the other suggestion is to
use different external tools.

00:55:31.420 --> 00:55:34.930
So if you make sure
that, for example,

00:55:34.930 --> 00:55:36.650
there is a package
manager that has

00:55:36.650 --> 00:55:39.060
all of the packages
available for you.

00:55:39.060 --> 00:55:41.850
There is a specified way
of deploying applications,

00:55:41.850 --> 00:55:44.460
[? so ?] that if, for
example, automate it,

00:55:44.460 --> 00:55:46.720
then the more you have--

00:55:46.720 --> 00:55:49.770
the more structure you
provide to your developers,

00:55:49.770 --> 00:55:53.430
the easier it will be for
them to find the right way

00:55:53.430 --> 00:55:54.340
to do things.

00:55:54.340 --> 00:55:57.420
So just creating, for
example, a definition

00:55:57.420 --> 00:56:01.620
of done, how things are done
within the organization, how

00:56:01.620 --> 00:56:04.620
you organize your work,
and sharing it with others,

00:56:04.620 --> 00:56:09.390
can be enough to actually
have a very good clean code

00:56:09.390 --> 00:56:10.860
within your different projects.

00:56:16.440 --> 00:56:19.170
OLGA MIERZWA-SULIMA: What
I think is also useful is

00:56:19.170 --> 00:56:22.530
to follow--

00:56:22.530 --> 00:56:28.740
so you can have a style guide
for structuring your code.

00:56:28.740 --> 00:56:33.270
But nothing stops you from
having a document which

00:56:33.270 --> 00:56:36.480
also describes the
best practices of how

00:56:36.480 --> 00:56:38.880
to structure your projects.

00:56:38.880 --> 00:56:42.960
And if you see, a
repetitive pattern,

00:56:42.960 --> 00:56:48.510
you can extract this pattern
and create a template

00:56:48.510 --> 00:56:53.400
from which you will
initialize every new project.

00:56:53.400 --> 00:56:57.780
So basically, the knowledge
that you accumulated

00:56:57.780 --> 00:57:02.390
in the past projects can
be quite easily transferred

00:57:02.390 --> 00:57:04.600
to a new project.

00:57:04.600 --> 00:57:07.280
So this is something
from my side.

00:57:07.280 --> 00:57:08.400
SAM: That's great.

00:57:08.400 --> 00:57:10.658
Thank you all so much for
answering these questions.

00:57:10.658 --> 00:57:11.450
Thank you everyone.

00:57:11.450 --> 00:57:12.905
OLGA MIERZWA-SULIMA:
Thanks, everyone.

00:57:12.905 --> 00:57:13.863
PEDRO SILVA: Thank you.

00:57:13.863 --> 00:57:16.310
ALEX K. GOLD: Thanks, everybody.

